  You know what’s harder than building an arc reactor? Teaching an AI to tell stories without making it sound like it’s reading a grocery list. \[May be building an arc reactor is hard I don’t know you are getting the point I hope\]But hey, I’ve always been partial to impossible projects — keeps life interesting.
  
  Press enter or click to view image in full size
  
  ![](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*SeDeCoSC5G75fTk_rBI3Pw.png)
  
  From Iron Man - I
  
  ## Where It All Started
  
  So there I was, staring at my screen, thinking “I’m going to create the perfect prompt for a storytelling chatbot.” Simple enough, right? Wrong. Dead wrong. What started as a simple idea turned into a challenge that had me questioning my life choices at 2 AM.
  
  First things first — I knew I needed structure. So I divided my approach into two clear segments: **Positive Prompt** and **Negative Prompt**. The positive prompt would tell the AI what to do, and the negative would warn it what to avoid. Think of it as giving directions to someone who’s never driven before — you need both “turn left here” and “don’t drive into that lake.”
  
  ## The Research
  
  I read a lot of articles and watched a bunch of YouTube videos, and this is what I discovered: technically, my prompt needs to have some limitations, such as a set of guidelines that bind it to accomplishing great things because it isn’t a person discussing a single incident; rather, it’s a story involving several things, and it won’t speak to you the way I do with you who are reading this blog.
  
  It needed to craft actual stories — narratives with beginnings, middles, ends, conflicts, and resolutions. Stories that would grab readers and not let go. This realization led me to the next phase, where I had to think like both a storyteller and a programmer.
  
  ## The Dancing Technique Discovery
  
  The first breakthrough came from YouTuber Kallaway (shoutout to him!). His “Dancing technique” was exactly what I needed — the concept of conflict and context dancing together throughout a story. I incorporated this philosophy right into my prompt:
  
  Press enter or click to view image in full size
  
  ![](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*nEJyGc3dnp7zGOs5a-cqXw.png)
  
  From How To Become A Master Storyteller by kallaway
  
  _“You are a narrative story generative chatbot, whose goal is to take the query of the user and the provided summary it has a random story content that can include various elements. Feel free to be creative and add your own twist, while ensuring the story progresses in a manner that reveals the conflict and then the context that resolves it, layer by layer like an onion.”_
  
  This became the foundation — stories that breathe, that reveal themselves gradually rather than dumping everything at once.
  
  ## Building the Foundation
  
  The second major component came from Philip Humm’s insights, combined with my own experimentation. I created a specific story database for content (which I’ll link it [**here**](https://medium.com/@karthikeyasuppa01/crafting-tales-with-ai-building-a-story-generator-using-rag-6aab06ba36fc)), but more importantly, I established the pre-writing framework:
  
  ” _In creating the story, keep in mind the following aspects of pre-writing:_
  
  1. _Concept and Theme: Develop a core idea or theme that the story will explore._
  2. _Character Development: Create well-rounded characters with distinct personalities, motivations, flaws, and goals._
  3. _Setting: Establish a believable environment, considering time period, location, and atmosphere._
  4. _Plot Outline: Map out the major events of your story, including the inciting incident, rising action, climax, falling action, and resolution._”
  
  ## The Technical Deep Dive
  
  From Kallaway’s storytelling masterclass, I learned about the “but and therefore” framework instead of “and then” — this creates those crucial conflict loops that keep readers hooked. From Philip Humm’s 9-minute storytelling guide, I discovered the power of zooming into moments rather than summarizing events.
  
  ![](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:490/1*sWu-O6a2HITQpJ6BC8_blg.png)
  
  I combined these insights into specific writing elements for my prompt:cha
  
  ## Get Karthikeya Suppa’s stories in your inbox
  
  Join Medium for free to get updates from this writer.
  
  Subscribe
  
  Subscribe
  
  Remember me for faster sign in
  
  _”When writing the story, incorporate the following elements:_
  
  1. _Hooking Introduction: Start with an engaging opening line or scene that captures the reader’s attention and introduces the main conflict._
  2. _Dialogue: Use realistic dialogue to reveal character traits and advance the plot._
  3. _Show, Don’t Tell: Describe details vividly using sensory language to immerse the reader in the story rather than simply stating facts._
  4. _Conflict and Tension: Build suspense by creating obstacles and challenges for your characters, raising the stakes as the story progresses._
  5. _Pacing: Balance action and exposition to maintain a steady rhythm throughout the story._
  6. _Point of View: Choose a consistent narrative perspective to guide the reader’s experience.”_
  
  ## The Story Structure Requirements
  
  I wasn’t done yet. I needed to ensure every story had proper structure:
  
  Press enter or click to view image in full size![](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1919/1*5c9LkD4esvoiE0mOJu9BMQ.png)
  
  Press enter or click to view image in full size![](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:1919/1*lFik2TLbMI4fSvGribPLng.png)
  
  _”Ensure the story includes the following key elements:_
  
  1. _Character Arc: Show how the protagonist develops and changes throughout the story in response to the conflict._
  2. _Climax: The turning point of the story where the central conflict reaches its peak._
  3. _Resolution: The conclusion where the conflicts are resolved, leaving the reader with a sense of satisfaction.”_
  
  ## The Final Polish
  
  The last layer focused on consistency and quality:
  
  ”Lastly, consider the following important aspects:
  
  1. Consistency: Ensure the characters, setting, and plot remain consistent throughout the story.
  2. Voice: Develop a unique writing style that reflects the tone and perspective of your story.
  3. Revision and Editing: Once the first draft is complete, thoroughly revise and edit to polish your writing, correct errors, and improve clarity.”
  
  ## The Magic Touch
  
  Here’s something I discovered through testing — the introduction matters immensely. I ended up including this specific intro line: _“I’d be delighted to spin a yarn for you! Here’s the story”_
  
  This simple phrase does something magical — it primes the reader’s imagination, making them more receptive to the story that follows.
  
  I also considered adding a **negative prompt** to avoid generic responses like “Here is your story,” but ultimately decided the positive framework was strong enough on its own.
  
  ## The Obsessive Details
  
  One final note that saved me countless hours: I made absolutely sure there were no spelling or grammatical mistakes in the prompt itself. Why? Because errors force the AI to spend processing power correcting and understanding mistakes instead of focusing on storytelling. Clean prompts equal better performance. Though not particularly significant, it is absolutely needed to hive better result.
  
  ## Where I Ended Up
  
  What started as a simple idea to create a storytelling chatbot turned into a comprehensive prompt engineering project a side with the other things. I now have a system that doesn’t just generate stories — it creates narratives that understand conflict, context, character development, and reader engagement.
  
  The journey taught me that good prompts aren’t just instructions; they’re architectural blueprints for creativity. Every word matters, every structure serves a purpose, and every technique builds toward one goal: stories that make people stop scrolling and start reading.
  
  Now, whenever I input a simple query into my chatbot, I get back stories that dance between conflict and context, stories that zoom into moments rather than summarize events, and stories that leave readers satisfied rather than confused.
  
  The data it received, the pacing tone, emotions, and relations etc are not the only things that count .The user’s inventiveness in describing what he wants to develop produces a structure where the AI fills in rest of the gaps with the creative architecture that I created , assisting it in securing the light it needs. [_This is how it turned out_](https://medium.com/@karthikeyasuppa01/once-upon-an-ai-spell-how-ai-turns-words-into-worlds-c3ce4c2e4a02)
  
  From chaos to character — that’s how you build a storytelling machine that actually knows how to tell a story.
  
